※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=36 diag=none

※ A tuple is several values travelling as one, written between `〈` and `〉`.
※ A product with no names, which is what makes it the right thing for a
※ function that answers with two of something: naming the parts of an answer
※ that is taken apart on the spot would be naming something that does not
※ outlive the line it is written on.
※
※ Names written next to each other take one apart, one name per member -- the
※ same thing `auto[a, b]` and `std::tie` do in C++, with nothing written to
※ say so beyond the comma.

let a: mut u8 = 3u8
let b: mut u8 = 9u8

fn both(x: u8, y: u8) → 〈u8, u8〉:
    〈x + y, x × y〉

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    ※ the type of the names comes from what the call answered with
    let sum, product := both(a, b)
    ※ and a tuple may be written down and named like any other value
    let pair: 〈u8, u8〉 = 〈1u8, 2u8〉
    let one, two := pair
    ※ 12 + 27 - 1 - 2
    sum + product - one - two
